faq:
  - q: "What is Tesla's hiring process in 2026?"
    a: "Tesla's hiring process is faster and less structured than most large tech companies. Typical flow: (1) Recruiter screen — 20–30 minutes, background and role fit. (2) Hiring manager interview — 45–60 minutes, technical or operational depth plus fit. (3) Technical assessment — for engineering roles, a take-home project or live technical session; for manufacturing and operations, a case-based problem. (4) Final loop — 2–4 interviews with team members and senior leadership, often compressed into a single day. Tesla moves fast — expect decisions in 2–4 weeks rather than 6–8. The culture is results-oriented and the interview is correspondingly direct: what have you built, what broke, what did you do about it."
  - q: "What does Tesla look for in candidates?"
    a: "Tesla looks for first-principles thinkers who can execute at speed in ambiguous environments. Key signals: (1) Bias to action — Tesla culture rewards people who move fast and iterate; candidates who describe lengthy deliberation without results struggle. (2) Engineering depth — Tesla is fundamentally a manufacturing and engineering company, not a software company. Real hands-on experience with hardware, systems, or manufacturing processes is valued at a premium. (3) Ownership — Tesla's headcount is lean relative to output. They hire people who own problems end-to-end, not people who hand off. (4) High pain tolerance — Tesla's culture is demanding. Candidates who present realistic experience with high-pressure, high-output environments credibly outperform those who cannot."
  - q: "How does Tesla engineering recruiting work?"
    a: "Tesla engineering covers Autopilot/FSD AI, hardware engineering, manufacturing engineering, energy systems, and software. For software and AI roles: recruiter screen → coding or technical take-home → hiring manager technical interview → loop with 2–3 engineers. For hardware and systems: recruiter screen → design exercise or portfolio review → engineering panel. Autopilot and FSD teams have the highest technical bar — they use neural network architecture and real-world deployment questions. Manufacturing engineering roles emphasize process optimization, Six Sigma thinking, and hands-on factory experience. Tesla does not follow a standard LeetCode-heavy format — practical, applied problems are more common."
  - q: "What are Tesla Autopilot and FSD roles like to get into?"
    a: "Tesla's Autopilot and Full Self-Driving (FSD) teams are some of the most competitive AI/ML roles in the industry. Strong candidates have: deep experience with computer vision, neural network training at scale, or embedded systems development. A background in robotics, autonomous vehicles, or perception systems is a significant advantage. Tesla's AI team does not use transformer-only approaches — candidates who understand diverse architectures (CNNs, video prediction, 3D spatial reasoning) are better positioned. The team runs a fleet of millions of vehicles producing real-world training data, so experience with large-scale data pipelines and real-time inference is directly relevant."
  - q: "What does Tesla pay in 2026?"
    a: "Tesla compensation is below peak FAANG in total comp but competitive for hardware and automotive tech. Software Engineer (L4-equivalent): $150–220K TC base-heavy, limited RSUs. Senior Software Engineer: $200–280K TC. Staff/Principal Engineer: $280–400K TC. Autopilot/AI roles pay a premium — senior AI engineer roles can reach $350–500K TC with meaningful equity upside tied to Tesla stock. Manufacturing and operations roles pay less: $80–130K for production roles, $120–200K for senior manufacturing engineers. Tesla does not offer the same equity scale as pre-IPO companies, but TSLA stock appreciation has delivered significant realized gains for long-tenured employees."
  - q: "How do I stand out applying to Tesla?"
    a: "Tesla receives enormous application volume — differentiation at the resume screen matters. Tactics that work: (1) Demonstrate first-principles problem solving in your resume — quantify what you built, broke, and improved, not just job responsibilities. (2) Understand Tesla's actual technical challenges: FSD reliability, energy storage cost reduction, Gigafactory throughput. Reference these in cover letters and interviews. (3) Emphasize speed and output over process — Tesla culture values what you shipped, not how many meetings you ran. (4) For engineering roles, personal projects, GitHub portfolios, or published work in relevant domains (embedded systems, CV, power electronics) signal genuine passion. Referrals from current Tesla engineers significantly improve application visibility."
