      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Tesla's culture and what it means for hiring</h2>
      <p>Tesla is not a conventional tech company. It is a manufacturing, energy, and AI company operating at a pace that most organizations cannot match. Understanding this shapes how to interview effectively.</p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Move fast, break things — then fix them.</strong> Tesla's culture rewards iteration speed. In interviews, stories that show quick action, failure, and rapid course correction land significantly better than stories about careful planning and flawless execution.</li>
        <li><strong>Headcount is lean by design.</strong> Tesla runs with fewer people than competitors at the same output level. This means every hire needs to cover more ground. In interviews, demonstrate breadth alongside depth — show that you can own problems outside your strict job description.</li>
        <li><strong>Mission connection matters.</strong> Tesla's stated mission is accelerating the world's transition to sustainable energy. Candidates who can articulate a genuine connection to this — not just interest in cars or tech — perform better in fit conversations with senior leaders.</li>
        <li><strong>Direct communication is the norm.</strong> Tesla interviews are blunt. Interviewers will push back on your answers. Practice defending your positions with evidence while updating when presented with valid counterpoints — the ability to hold and revise views under pressure is explicitly evaluated.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Role-specific preparation by function</h2>
      <h3>Autopilot / AI / FSD</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Deep computer vision and neural network knowledge — architecture tradeoffs, training pipelines, real-time inference constraints</li>
        <li>Understand Tesla's data engine approach: how fleet data is used for training and what distinguishes it from lidar-dependent competitors</li>
        <li>Prepare to discuss specific failure modes in autonomous systems and how you would debug them at scale</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Software Engineering (Product / Infra)</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Applied problem-solving over LeetCode grinding — Tesla favors practical questions about systems you've built</li>
        <li>System design for reliability and low-latency applications relevant to vehicle software or energy systems</li>
        <li>Embedded C/C++ is relevant for firmware and ECU roles — different preparation than web/backend FAANG interviews</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Manufacturing Engineering / Operations</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Gigafactory throughput, cell production, and supply chain constraints are frequent interview topics</li>
        <li>Prepare process improvement stories: what was broken, what you changed, what the measured result was</li>
        <li>Six Sigma, lean manufacturing, and DFM (Design for Manufacturability) knowledge signals credibility</li>
      </ul>
